All eleven required H2 headings present in order; declared counts match the document.

**Saved artifact:** `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Marcus-Aurelius/2026-08-27_020004/fan.md`

**Verdict:** `revise` — trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit. **2 blockers, 9 concerns.**

**Strongest hit (FAN-H3):** the Book 1 debt-ledger read — *"A man who believes every good thing in him is borrowed experiences rest as spending money he did not earn, while the lenders are dead and cannot be repaid."* I knew Book 1 was seventeen entries of gratitude and I knew about the paperwork at the circus games; I had never seen them connected, and once connected they don't come apart. That plus opening on Fronto's polecat letter instead of a marble-bust quotation is why this clears the fan bar.

**Highest-priority concern (FAN-R2, blocker):** the page never names **Junius Rusticus**. The sentence *"Marcus found the Stoics, through the man he thanks for it in his notebook"* leaves "the man" unnamed while Fronto is named twice in the two preceding sentences — so the referent chain hands the Epictetus credit to Fronto, the reverse of the fact (Med. 1.7 is the "From Rusticus" entry; Fronto's is 1.11). It sits inside the page's origin claim and nothing later corrects it. The repair is one clause — and naming him unlocks the review's best additive find: that same Rusticus was urban prefect of Rome 162–168 and presided over the trial that executed Justin Martyr, connecting the two halves the article already covers separately.

The other blocker (FAN-R1) is the Avidius Cassius slur, which is one quotation welded from two *Historia Augusta* documents by two speakers thirteen years apart, with "philosophical old woman" misdated to 175 when it comes from a Lucius Verus letter that must predate his death in early 169.
